86 17592186045417 true] [17592186413390 1079 #uuid "a2554085-77fe-5157-9ee0-fb327d6f9788" 13194139534186 17592186045417 true] [17592186413391 1075 #edb/ref 17592186413358 13194139534186 17592186045417 true] [17592186413391 1075 #edb/ref 17592186413375 13194139534186 17592186045417 true] [17592186413391 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139534186 17592186045417 true] [17592186413391 1077 "field-2" 13194139534186 17592186045417 true] [17592186413391 1078 #edb/ref 17592186413358 13194139534186 17592186045417 true] [17592186413391 1079 #uuid "db85f0d1-90c6-55e8-8218-c8897868d5ec" 13194139534186 17592186045417 true] [17592186413392 1075 #edb/ref 17592186413356 13194139534186 17592186045417 true] [17592186413392 1075 #edb/ref 17592186413362 13194139534186 17592186045417 true] [17592186413392 1075 #edb/ref 17592186413370 13194139534186 17592186045417 true] [17592186413392 1075 #edb/ref 17592186413376 13194139534186 17592186045417 true] [17592186413392 1075 #edb/ref 17592186413384 13194139534186 17592186045417 true] [17592186413392 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139534186 17592186045417 true] [17592186413392 1077 "field-1" 13194139534186 17592186045417 true] [17592186413392 1078 #edb/ref 17592186413356 13194139534186 17592186045417 true] [17592186413392 1079 #uuid "fe88487e-f897-504c-b0b7-1ea54245c515" 13194139534186 17592186045417 true] [17592186413393 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413385 13194139534186 17592186045417 true] [17592186413393 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413391 13194139534186 17592186045417 true] [17592186413393 1069 "q-340679" 13194139534186 17592186045417 true] [17592186413393 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534186 17592186045417 true] [17592186413393 1071 "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ defined above, select the correct options below." 13194139534186 17592186045417 true] [17592186413393 1072 #uuid "0c2afbe9-632c-56a2-8877-4a2cf3a61d97" 13194139534186 17592186045417 true] [17592186413393 1074 "We begin by using the product-to-sum identity\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)+\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-7\\pi ))+\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3\\pi )) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}(-1-(-1))+\\frac{1}{6}(-1-(-1)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)$ $\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product." 13194139534186 17592186045417 true] [17592186413394 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413388 13194139534186 17592186045417 true] [17592186413394 1069 "q-340515" 13194139534186 17592186045417 true] [17592186413394 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534186 17592186045417 true] [17592186413394 1071 "Let $F(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x+2\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" 13194139534186 17592186045417 true] [17592186413394 1072 #uuid "4d1f9132-9d3c-569e-af11-701e82cf296a" 13194139534186 17592186045417 true] [17592186413394 1074 "Since $f$ is the $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have \n\n- $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodicity:\n\n$$\nf(x)=F(x)\n$$\n\n \nfor all $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that $F$ itself is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic, because for any $x\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x+2\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) \\\\  & =F(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $F$ already satisfies the periodicity rule, the $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic extension agrees with $F$ everywhere, so $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $f$ has period $\\frac{\\pi }{2},$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+2\\pi )=f(x+4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139534186 17592186045417 true] [17592186413395 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413389 13194139534186 17592186045417 true] [17592186413395 1069 "q-340506" 13194139534186 17592186045417 true] [17592186413395 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534186 17592186045417 true] [17592186413395 1071 "Let $G(x)=x+1$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $g(x)$ is the $4$-periodic extension of $G(x)$ from $x\\in [0,4)$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $g(x+8)=g(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $g(x)=G(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $g(x+2)=g(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" 13194139534186 17592186045417 true] [17592186413395 1072 #uuid "e155a024-962b-5f82-95d5-406e9c9d0535" 13194139534186 17592186045417 true] [17592186413395 1074 "Since $g$ is the $4$-periodic extension of $G,$ we have \n\n- $g(x+4)=g(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $g(x)=G(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,4).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x+8)=g((x+4)+4)=g(x+4)=g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=5.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(5)=g(1+4)=g(1)=G(1)=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G(5)=5+1=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $g(x)\\ne G(x)$ for $x=5.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=1.$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1+2)=g(3)=G(3)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1)=G(1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $g(x+2)\\ne g(x)$ for $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139534186 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-01205902-7ed5-5562-9748-9119a799b0e3" 17592186413351 "answer-05e1e707-efff-528a-b5df-65f3a2dfbd42" 17592186413352 "answer-18a59817-6dfb-5331-b664-4eed5ae288bd" 17592186413353 "answer-281d8718-96cc-5786-9125-bdda8d8dfa5d" 17592186413354 "answer-4ef2209e-3d8f-5297-ad7a-edc869e85aa5" 17592186413355 "answer-5cdcf984-3000-5dc4-be61-ef3673edd14c" 17592186413356 "answer-5ed25c1f-08d5-5b38-8bf8-b0a0bfd412ea" 17592186413357 "answer-69cbe4e6-0948-5e9f-9176-a7c91f104c4c" 17592186413358 "answer-6ebb288a-d478-5621-bc91-6939d6730dab" 17592186413359 "answer-734b3ad1-94c6-5ccc-a8b8-d77fb92327cb" 17592186413360 "answer-74949511-a7ef-5835-b1ed-11c9565189c6" 17592186413361 "answer-7d216d93-03a2-52c7-8109-b053ea99a0b7" 17592186413362 "answer-83094832-5120-528d-afe1-e8471b128d10" 17592186413363 "answer-88d440e0-29f7-5376-a757-92d36791c9cd" 17592186413364 "answer-8ecad901-e457-5a7f-9933-d49a111a2f39" 17592186413365 "answer-9053ae09-1967-50b2-bec7-285800590fb1" 17592186413366 "answer-9a63d038-bac5-5072-83a9-d1a2575602e5" 17592186413367 "answer-a32e9ce1-165d-5035-b70b-2b4a5c8fee66" 17592186413368 "answer-b4aa1927-cea8-52e9-87f5-49a49eb90e82" 17592186413369 "answer-c04eff61-d1a2-5922-831e-05e57e1cedbe" 17592186413370 "answer-c052414d-62b2-5ddf-8d38-b8519a51bb8d" 17592186413371 "answer-c163715f-e3af-5949-bcaf-b925167883f9" 17592186413372 "answer-c1c02e86-9fa1-5e41-9588-96d01350f6b0" 17592186413373 "answer-caa70edf-67db-5711-80d1-9a6c3409bbee" 17592186413374 "answer-cd85ba26-4